<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Leidang</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as leiðangr, leding, ledung, lething, expeditio</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The leidang (Old Norse leiðangr) was a form of conscription in medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scandinavia">Scandinavia</a> that raised coastal fleets for seasonal expeditions and the defence of the realm. The Icelandic sagas link its introduction to King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haakon-the-good">Haakon I of Norway</a> in the 10th century, and it is first mentioned in 985 by the court poets of Jarl Haakon and his son Erik, though its age is disputed: Niels <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lund">Lund</a> held that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> had no real leiðangr until 1170. The land was divided into ship districts (skipreiða), each bound to build, equip and crew a ship for two or three months; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> had 279 in 1277. From the 12th and 13th centuries it became in many places a tax, paid by free farmers until the 19th century.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Kings raised fleets through the <b>leidang</b>, a levy in which coastal districts each equipped and crewed a warship, and the skaldic poems celebrate sea battles such as Hafrsfjord, Svolder in 1000 and Nesjar in 1016, where Olaf Haraldsson defeated Earl Svein Haakonsson.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-norway">Kingdom of Norway · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Kings kept armed retinues, called out the ship levy (<b>leiðangr</b>) and spread the new faith, sometimes by force, and Cnut the Great ruled England, Denmark and Norway together.</blockquote><a href="/societies/vikings">Social Hierarchy of the Vikings</a></li></ul></section>
